<header><p class="st-kicker">county · France and the Low Countries</p><h1 class="st-title">County of Bigorre</h1><p class="st-meta">800 CE – 1607 CE (approx.) · capital Tarbes</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Small Pyrenean county around Tarbes and Lourdes, remembered for one of the oldest charters of customs in France.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Bigorre, the land of the ancient <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/bigerriones">Bigerriones</a>, became a county under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carolingian-empire">Carolingians</a> in the 9th century, at the edge of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duchy-of-gascony">Duchy of Gascony</a>. Its first counts are poorly documented, and their line was linked with the rulers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gascony">Gascony</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-aragon">Aragon</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The county controlled the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adour">Adour</a> valley and passes into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-aragon">Aragon</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarbes">Tarbes</a> as its seat and the castle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lourdes">Lourdes</a> as its stronghold. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fors-de-bigorre">Fors de Bigorre</a>, granted around 1097, set out the rights of the inhabitants and limits on comital power. In the 13th century Countess <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/petronilla-countess-of-bigorre">Petronilla</a> married five times, leaving a bitter succession dispute.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Bigorre passed through several hands, including English rule after 1360, before falling to the House of Foix-Béarn in the 15th century and then to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-albret">Albret</a> kings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-navarre">Navarre</a>. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-iv-of-france">Henry of Navarre</a> became Henry IV of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, it was united with the crown in 1607.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/county-of-bigorre.jpg" alt="County of Bigorre" width="320" height="352"><figcaption>Image: Syryatsu · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Blason_d%C3%A9partement_fr_Hautes-Pyr%C3%A9n%C3%A9es.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>800 CE – 1607 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>county</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Tarbes</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Petronilla, Henry IV</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Gascon Occitan</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/france">France</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Bigorre</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1204&amp;focus=county-of-bigorre" aria-label="County of Bigorre on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4682 644 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where County of Bigorre lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5002 900L4986 849M5002 900L5039 908M5002 900L5054 728"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4986 849h0M5039 908h0M5054 728h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5002 900h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5002 900h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Tarbes. <a href="/atlas?year=1204&amp;focus=county-of-bigorre">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
